<p>U.S. President Donald Trump convened the first meeting of a new, U.S.-led “Board of Peace” focused on stabilizing and rebuilding Gaza, welcoming visiting delegations and leaders to Washington, DC. At the meeting, Trump said the United States would commit $10 billion, while participating countries announced additional pledges totaling more than $6.5 billion (often described as about $7 billion) for Gaza relief and reconstruction. Several governments in the Middle East and beyond paired funding offers with proposed security contributions. Reports cited pledges of at least $1 billion each from Qatar, Saudi Arabia and the United Arab Emirates, and said Morocco signaled it was ready to provide police or officers for an international stabilization effort in Gaza. The board’s goals still sit alongside big open questions about governance, representation and disarmament: Trump said it “looks like” Hamas will disarm, while other coverage noted Hamas has shown little willingness to do so, and that the board’s mission—and its relationship to the United Nations—has drawn scrutiny, including from traditional Western allies that have stayed away or limited participation.</p><img src='https://kagiproxy.com/img/1CTIXbphdn0R8_hD6NOo9NcmEW_rfPYtjl2iIM8gb4n-EzskNXd0hu1pLgV1_HwfFyj78lTf7xh7m6QkUkdsGiboWt9PFR2uP9XIJhcAJMZs6pQbQrRHkuhe7D5BcMmV3c7CfIcC0FVCZHZakADBHR672Xnr4wpWJYAEBRxaaxnXhoIvcg' alt='US President Donald Trump during speeches at the Board of Peace inaugural meeting in Washington, DC, on February 19, 2026.' /><br /><h3>Highlights:</h3><ul><li>Who showed up: More than two dozen countries joined the first meeting in Washington, though some major, traditional U.S. allies kept their distance or declined full membership.</li><li>Security offers: Coverage described a proposed multinational force or stabilization mission that could include troops from Albania, Indonesia, Kazakhstan, Kosovo and Morocco.</li><li>India’s role: India attended as an observer and was represented by Chargé d’Affaires Namgya Khampa, according to reports citing the participants list.</li><li>UN relationship: Trump said the board would work with the United Nations, while also suggesting it would “almost be looking over” the UN—a framing Politico noted is not provided for in the UN charter.</li><li>Iran timeline: Semafor reported Trump also said he could decide within 10 days whether to strike Iran, as other coverage tied the board’s debut to rising U.S.-Iran tensions.</li></ul><h3>Perspectives:</h3><ul><li>White House/President Trump: Trump said the U.S. would commit $10 billion to the Board of Peace effort and suggested Hamas would disarm, presenting the initiative as a major international grouping. (<a href='https://www.pbs.org/newshour/show/trump-says-u-s-will-give-10-billion-to-board-of-peace-promising-to-rebuild-gaza'>PBS NewsHour</a>)</li><li>United Arab Emirates: UAE Foreign Minister Sheikh Abdullah bin Zayed al-Nahyan said the UAE would pledge $1.2 billion to the board, according to a Reuters factbox. (<a href='https://www.al-monitor.com/originals/2026/02/factbox-contributions-gaza-board-peace-members'>Al-Monitor (Reuters)</a>)</li><li>Qatar: Qatar’s Emir Sheikh Tamim bin Hamad Al-Thani pledged $1 billion to the board, according to a Reuters factbox. (<a href='https://www.al-monitor.com/originals/2026/02/factbox-contributions-gaza-board-peace-members'>Al-Monitor (Reuters)</a>)</li><li>Traditional Western allies/critics: Politico reported skepticism from Democrats, Europeans, the United Nations and Palestinians, and said some countries were deferring to the White House on rebuilding plans despite limited clarity on details. (<a href='https://www.politico.com/news/2026/02/19/trump-gets-his-board-of-peace-even-as-bigger-countries-steer-clear-00789617'>Politico</a>)</li></ul><h3>Sources:</h3><ul><li><a href='https://www.bbc.com/news/articles/cp8rke05kv4o'>Trump&#x27;s Board of Peace members pledge $7bn in Gaza relief</a> - bbc.com</li><li><a href='https://www.dw.